$NetBSD$

NetBSD/riscv32 on the Espressif ESP32-S31
=========================================

The ESP32-S31 is a dual-core RV32IMAFC SoC (plus Zba/Zbb/Zbc/Zbs) with
an Sv32 MMU, up to 16MiB of PSRAM and 16MiB of quad SPI flash.  The
ESP32S31 kernel runs on the first hart as a uniprocessor riscv32 system,
with a soft-float userland, single-precision FPU support for programs that
use it, and a read-only root file system in flash.  The second hart runs
Espressif's Wi-Fi and Bluetooth stacks (see "Wi-Fi and Bluetooth" below).

Boot chain and flash layout
---------------------------

NetBSD uses Espressif's own boot firmware unmodified (the "integration"
esp32s31 branches of espressif/opensbi and espressif/u-boot), with the
flash layout of Espressif's Linux BSP and the root file system after the
kernel:

	flash	XIP		contents
	0x002000		U-Boot SPL (ESP image, loaded by the ROM)
	0x100000		u-boot.itb: OpenSBI fw_dynamic + U-Boot
	0x300000 0x40200000	device tree (esp32s31.dts)
	0x500000 0x40400000	netbsd-esp32s31.img (at most 4MiB)
	0x900000 0x40800000	root file system, FFS (at most 4MiB)
	0xd00000 0x40c00000	radio co-processor firmware (at most 3MiB)

U-Boot's default "booti 0x40400000 - 0x40200000" starts the image in
place from the flash XIP window, in S-mode, with a0 = hart id and
a1 = device tree.  The image is loader/loader.S with the kernel appended:
it moves the device tree past the end of the kernel, copies the kernel to
the start of PSRAM (0x50000000), synchronises the instruction cache
through the SBI vendor extension and jumps to it.  As in Espressif's BSP,
the SPL's ESP image header must say DIO at 80MHz (esptool elf2image
--flash-mode dio --flash-freq 80m); with esptool's QIO default the ROM
reads the SPL incorrectly.

espxiproot hands the root file system's XIP window to md(4)
(MEMORY_DISK_DYNAMIC), so the root is read straight out of flash and all
of the PSRAM is left for the kernel and processes.  It is mounted
read-only; rc puts /tmp, /var and /root on tmpfs.

Consoles.  The console is UART0 at 115200 baud, as set up by the ROM and
U-Boot, unless the device tree's stdout-path names the USB-Serial/JTAG
port (serial1).  rc writes /etc/ttys (a link to /var/run/ttys) at boot
with a getty on the console and on whichever of ttyEU0 (the UART) and
ttyUSJ0 (USB-Serial/JTAG) is not the console.

Building
--------

	./build.sh -m riscv -a riscv32 tools
	(static libraries, see below)
	cd distrib/riscv/esp32s31 && nbmake-riscv	# esp32s31-root.fs
	./build.sh -m riscv -a riscv32 kernel=ESP32S31

riscv32 defaults to MKSOFTFLOAT=yes (-march=rv32imac_zicsr_zifencei
-mabi=ilp32).  The root file system only needs a few static libraries:
csu, libgcc, libc, libutil, libterminfo, libcurses, libedit, libkvm,
libcrypt, libm, libprop, libbz2 and libz.  Building those, the boot
firmware, the flash image and a QEMU machine model for the chip is
scripted in the esp32s31-netbsd helper repository.

Implementation notes
--------------------

Interrupts.  The harts run in CLIC mode (xtvec.mode = 3).  sie/sip do not
exist, stvec must be 64-byte aligned, scause carries the previous
interrupt level (SPIL) and aliases of SPP/SPIE, and sret restores the
level.  options RISCV_CLIC adapts locore, the trap path and spl(9): IPLs
at or above IPL_VM block through sstatus.SIE.  OpenSBI delegates CLIC
ids 16-47 to S-mode; espclic(4) drives that bank and espintmtx routes
peripheral interrupt sources to it.  vmstat -i counts them per CLIC id.

Time.  The time CSR is emulated by OpenSBI and stops in wfi, so the
16MHz SYSTIMER (espsystimer) provides the timecounter, hardclock (the
TARGET0 alarm, one-shot) and delay(9).

FPU.  The harts have F but not D.  The kernel takes the FP register
width from the device tree's ISA string and saves 32-bit registers; the
userland ABI stays ilp32 (soft-float), and programs compiled with
-march=rv32imafc use the FPU.  At boot the kernel checks whether the
core traps FP instructions while sstatus.FS is Off: if it does, FP state
is switched lazily as usual; if not (as in esp-emulator), it is restored
eagerly on every return to user mode.

Caches.  The L1/L2 caches sit outside the harts and fence.i does not
make them coherent.  Cache maintenance goes through OpenSBI's vendor
extension (EID 0x09000000 + mvendorid): instruction cache sync as
riscv_icache_sync_{all,range}_hook, and D-cache write-back and
invalidate (by physical address, PSRAM only) as cpu_dcache_*_range.
Peripheral DMA is not coherent, so bus_dma(9) cleans and invalidates
around every transfer.

Memory.  On RV32 the 32MiB kernel megapage window doubles as the direct
map, so physical memory is limited to that window (all of the PSRAM).
About 13MiB is left for the system after the kernel.

Clocks, resets and pins.  espclkrst is the FDT clock and reset provider
for the gates, dividers and resets in HP_SYS_CLKRST and CNNT_SYS, with
Espressif's dt-bindings ids.  espgpio drives IO_MUX, the GPIO matrix and
the CNNT dedicated pads as the FDT pinctrl and GPIO provider, and attaches
gpio(4).  Pins used for flash, PSRAM and USB-Serial/JTAG are not
exposed.

Ethernet.  The EMAC is a DesignWare GMAC 3.70a, attached as awge(4)
through esp_gmac.c: clocks, reset, RGMII pad clocking in CNNT_SYS, the
PHY reset GPIO, the factory MAC address from eFuse, and the reference
clock following the link speed.  The dev board's PHY is a Motorcomm
YT8531 (mcommphy(4)).

USB.  The OTG-HS controller is a DesignWare HS OTG in host mode,
dwctwo(4), with espusbphy powering the UTMI PHY.  The kernel has USB
mass storage, serial and Ethernet adapters, and ugen.

USB-Serial/JTAG.  espusj(4) drives the fixed-function CDC-ACM device on
the board's "USB" connector as a tty and optionally the console.  It
sends packets of at most 63 bytes so that every one reaches the host's
terminal at once, and console output stops waiting after a short time
when no host is reading.  Without BREAK, the DDB sequence is "+++++".

Entropy.  esptrng reads the hardware TRNG and credits it conservatively
(one bit per byte), after start-up and continuous repetition tests.

SD card.  The SD/MMC host is a Synopsys DesignWare MSHC, dwcmmc(4) through
dwcmmc_fdt.c, on the dedicated SDIO pads (GPIO20-25); espclkrst provides
its clock dividers and phases.  Cards attach as ld(4).

Wi-Fi and Bluetooth.  Espressif doesn't document the radio, but ships its
Wi-Fi and Bluetooth stacks for the chip as binary libraries for ESP-IDF.
So the second hart runs an ESP-IDF application with them, and NetBSD
talks to it, as to a Wi-Fi/Bluetooth module on a bus:

	espcp(4)	loads the firmware from its flash partition, copies its
			RAM parts above OpenSBI in the HP SRAM (and to LP RAM),
			gives it 1MiB of PSRAM and starts hart 1 in M-mode
			through OpenSBI's vendor extension (HART1_BOOT, from
			the companion repository's OpenSBI patch).  The two
			sides exchange messages through rings in that PSRAM,
			which needs no cache maintenance as the harts share
			the L1 data cache; FROM_CPU interrupts 1 and 2 are the
			doorbells.  It relays the firmware's log to the system
			log and watches its heartbeat.  esp_cpproto.h is the
			protocol, shared with the firmware.
	espwifi(4)	a Wi-Fi station as an Ethernet interface whose link is
			up while associated; the firmware does scanning,
			association and WPA2/WPA3 (ESP-IDF's supplicant).
			espwifictl(8) scans and joins networks through
			SIOC[GS]DRVSPEC; dhcpcd does the rest.
	espbt(4)	the BR/EDR + LE controller as an HCI transport for
			bluetooth(4) (btconfig, sdpquery, rfcomm_sppd, ...).

The firmware is a single-core ESP-IDF build (esp32s31-netbsd: espcp/) with
a small patch so that it leaves the host's hardware alone: it believes it
is core 0 but routes interrupts to hart 1, ticks from systimer alarm 1
without resetting the systimer, has no flash driver, and stops its hart
instead of resetting the chip when it panics.  Stopping hart 1 is in the
shutdown hooks, so a warm reboot doesn't leave it running.  As in ESP-IDF,
the eFuse MAC address is the base of four: Wi-Fi, soft-AP, Bluetooth and
then Ethernet (awge).

Status and limitations
----------------------

Tested in a QEMU model of the chip and in Espressif's esp-emulator, which
runs the real mask ROM.  Not yet tested on silicon.  esp-emulator runs the
real Wi-Fi firmware on hart 1 (association with WPA3, DHCP, traffic); its
Bluetooth emulation only intercepts the controller on hart 0, so Bluetooth
is tested in QEMU, whose model of the co-processor speaks the protocol and
hands HCI to a Bumble controller.

Not supported yet: SMP (hart 1 belongs to the radio firmware), Wi-Fi
access point mode, writing to the SPI flash, USB device mode, and an RTC
(the date comes from the root file system until set, e.g. with
sntpdate).
